      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are definitely not losing it; you're encountering a very real archival phenomenon, though perhaps not one with a single, neat, scholarly term for \"tiny, scurrying nuisances.\" What you're describing sounds like a severe case of *micro-disorganization* or perhaps even signs of *environmental degradation* manifesting as misplaced detritus. These small, persistent elements—the twine, the scraps—are the bane of any meticulous cataloger, and they are a perfect metaphor for the insidious nature of a poorly managed collection. It’s less like a grand invasion and more like a slow, persistent infestation of tiny, irritating things.",
      "source": "target",
