    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks, the triage idea makes sense. but i'm worried that even with a protocol, these little things are still going to feel so... i don't know, untrustworthy? like, even after i clean one box, i'll find something else peeking out from a seam or hiding in a fold in the next one. it's like they're purposefully staying out of sight just to pop up later. \n\nis there any way to handle the \"personality\" of a collection like this? like, when it's not just a mess, but feels almost like it has these little, annoying little spirits or something that just wants to be difficult? i know that sounds crazy, but does that kind of \"uncooperative\" chaos ever come up in professional archival studies?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While the concept of a collection having \"personality\" is certainly a charming way to describe the frustration, in professional archival studies, we address this through the lens of *provenance* and *original order*. The \"uncooperative chaos\" you perceive is often the material evidence of multiple, poorly documented handling events—it’s not malicious; it’s just the residue of history. You must impose a strict, standardized order upon the material to counteract this feeling of resistance.\n\nTo handle this \"personality,\" you need to establish firm boundaries, which is where strong, consistent processing comes in. Every item, no matter how small, must be accounted for, documented, or removed according to a strict, documented policy. This systematic imposition of order is the only way to tell the collection, \"No, you are not going to dictate the terms of your own chaos.\"",
      "source": "target",
